  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes electrified vehicle powertrain layouts and the main energy-storage formats used with them. Across the top, simplified car diagrams compare a pure battery electric vehicle with several hybrid and plug-in hybrid architectures: series, parallel, series–parallel, and a more complex hybrid arrangement. The battery or supercapacitor, electric motor, power converter, charger, fuel tank, fuel pump, and internal combustion engine are color coded, showing how the electric and combustion components are combined differently in each design. The BEV is the simplest, relying only on an electrical path from battery to motor and charger. The hybrid layouts add an engine and fuel system, with increasing mechanical and electrical coupling between engine and motor as the configurations move from series to parallel and mixed systems.\n\nThe lower left portion shows three common cell packaging formats for batteries or supercapacitors: cylindrical, prismatic, and pouch. The lower right diagram gives a layered view of an electric double-layer capacitor, identifying the electrode, active material, electrolyte, and separator. Overall, the figure links vehicle-level powertrain architectures with the physical forms and internal structure of electrochemical energy-storage devices.",
